      <h2 id="best-event-matchtrip">#1 — MatchTrip (Best Event-Centered Trip Planning)</h2>
      <p>MatchTrip is the only tool on this list that inverts the planning order. Instead of picking a city and checking what's on, <a href="https://matchtrip-blue.vercel.app" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">MatchTrip</a> starts from the events: discover major sporting events worldwide, pick one, and get a complete travel plan assembled around it — flights, hotels, tickets, and a city guide for the destination, with budget estimates that include the full trip rather than just the airfare.</p>
      <p>That inversion matters for how fans actually decide. The honest version of a fan trip is "I want to see Roma–Lazio in person" or "I want to be in the host city for the final" — the event is the constraint, and everything downstream (which weekend, which neighborhood, how much it all costs) is derived from it. MatchTrip's event discovery covers major events across 15+ host cities, so it also answers the earlier question fans scroll fixtures hoping to answer: which match is worth building a trip around? Because the tool is free to use, it works as a planning and comparison layer even when you eventually book elsewhere.</p>
      <p>The trade-off: an event-centered planner is a starting point, not the whole stack. For matchday logistics — which metro exit, where the away section gathers, how early to arrive — local knowledge and stadium information in the city guide get you oriented, but dedicated forums and the club's official guidance are still the last word. And like every young tool, its event coverage depth varies by sport and region.</p>
      <p>Best for: fans planning a trip where the event is the point — one big fixture, a tournament, or choosing which match to travel for in the first place.</p>

      <div class="product-box">
        <h2>MatchTrip</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Event discovery first</strong> — browse major sporting events worldwide, then build the trip</li>
