          <li><strong>Full-trip budget estimates</strong> — the real cost including tickets, not just travel</li>
          <li><strong>15+ host cities covered</strong> — with venue information and city context</li>
          <li><strong>Free to use</strong> — planning and comparison cost nothing</li>
        </ul>
        <a href="https://matchtrip-blue.vercel.app" class="btn" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Visit MatchTrip</a>
      </div>

      <h2 id="best-booking-google-travel">#2 — Google Travel (Best Booking &amp; Research Layer)</h2>
      <p>Once the event and dates are fixed, Google Travel is the strongest free layer for the travel legs. Flights shows date-grid pricing around the fixture, Hotels filters by the stadium's neighborhood instead of the city center, and Maps handles the two logistics questions that define matchday: how to get to the venue and where to eat before or after away from the crush. Google's review synthesis and live opening hours are fresher than any planner's static data.</p>
      <p>The trade-off: Google has no concept of the event. It will happily book you the cheapest flight that lands two hours after kickoff — the event-awareness layer has to come from you or from an event planner like MatchTrip.</p>
      <p>Best for: the booking and verification stage of every fan trip.</p>

      <div class="product-box">
        <h2>Google Travel</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Date-grid flight pricing</strong> — see the cost spread around the fixture</li>
          <li><strong>Neighborhood hotel search</strong> — stay near the venue, not the tourist center</li>
          <li><strong>Maps matchday logistics</strong> — transit routes and pre-match food</li>
          <li><strong>Fresh data</strong> — opening hours and closures that reflect reality</li>
          <li><strong>Free</strong></li>
        </ul>
        <a href="https://www.google.com/travel/" class="btn" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">Visit Google Travel</a>
      </div>

      <h2 id="best-city-tripadvisor">#3 — TripAdvisor (Best City Research)</h2>
      <p>For the non-match hours of a fan trip — the restaurant before the game, the sightseeing on the spare day, the review-backed answer to "is this tour worth it" — TripAdvisor's review corpus remains the deepest available. Its AI trip builder can draft the non-sporting half of the itinerary, and the restaurant coverage is strong precisely in the stadium-adjacent districts where you need it.</p>
      <p>The trade-off: TripAdvisor knows cities, not fixtures. It is a complement to the event layer, not a substitute.</p>
      <p>Best for: filling the rest of the weekend around the match. For full AI itinerary generation, see <a href="/reviews/best-ai-travel-planning-tools-2026/">our AI travel planner ranking</a>.</p>

      <div class="product-box">
        <h2>TripAdvisor</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Deepest review corpus</strong> — decades of first-hand traveler opinions</li>
          <li><strong>AI trip builder</strong> — draft the non-match itinerary in one prompt</li>
          <li><strong>Strong restaurant coverage</strong> — including stadium districts</li>
          <li><strong>Free with account</strong></li>
        </ul>
        <a href="https://www.tripadvisor.com" class="btn" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">Visit TripAdvisor</a>
      </div>

      <h2 id="best-platforms-booking">#4 — Booking Platforms (Best Flights &amp; Stays)</h2>
      <p>The booking platforms earn their place here for one reason: event travel rewards price discipline. Fixed dates mean no flexibility to exploit, and demand concentrates around match weekends in host cities, so the difference between searching well and badly is often significant. Skyscanner's whole-month and multi-city views find the workable arrival and departure windows; Booking.com's map-based filtering pins accommodation to the right district and shows what's actually left as inventory clears ahead of a big fixture; and the free-cancellation filter is worth using deliberately for events where ticket plans can shift.</p>
      <p>The trade-off: these platforms solve the search problem, not the coordination problem. The event date, the venue, the ticket, and the stay still have to be reconciled by you — or by an event-centered planner upstream. For a fuller treatment of the booking and planning landscape, see our <a href="/reviews/best-ai-travel-planning-tools-2026/">AI travel planning tools guide</a>.</p>
      <p>Best for: the actual transactions, with flexible-cancellation terms when the fixture is not yet guaranteed.</p>

      <div class="product-box">
        <h2>Booking Platforms</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Skyscanner</strong> — whole-month pricing and multi-city routing for event windows</li>
          <li><strong>Booking.com</strong> — map-first filtering near the venue, live availability</li>
          <li><strong>Free-cancellation filters</strong> — insurance against fixture changes</li>
          <li><strong>Price alerts</strong> — watch a route from before the fixture is confirmed</li>
        </ul>
        <a href="https://www.skyscanner.com" class="btn" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">Visit Skyscanner</a>
      </div>

      <h2 id="best-tickets-transport">#5 — Ticket &amp; Transport Tools (Best Matchday Layer)</h2>
      <p>Tickets are the leg of a fan trip with their own market dynamics, and the dedicated marketplaces handle them best. SeatGeek and StubHub aggregate secondary inventory with price history on individual listings — genuinely useful for judging whether today's asking price for a derby is normal or inflated. Official club channels remain the cheapest route when allocations open to your membership tier, so marketplaces are best treated as the comparison and fallback layer.</p>
      <p>Two cautions from collective fan experience. First, buy from platforms with guarantees and documented seat details, and be skeptical of prices far below the market — too-cheap match tickets are the most common fan-trip scam. Second, mind the tie between tickets and transport: a match that finishes late at night changes which hotel area and which morning flight are realistic. Venue information from the planning layer (MatchTrip's city guides, Google Maps) closes that loop.</p>
      <p>Best for: the ticket purchase, compared against official club pricing.</p>

